WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.319 --> 00:00:02.990
Electrophilic aromatic substitution is a

00:00:02.990 --> 00:00:05.250
reaction that replaces one of the

00:00:05.250 --> 00:00:09.080
hydrogen atoms on benzene with a group.

00:00:09.080 --> 00:00:10.980
Before we show the arrow pushing

00:00:10.980 --> 00:00:13.170
mechanism, let's dive into the reaction

00:00:13.170 --> 00:00:15.240
flask and see what's going on in there.

00:00:15.240 --> 00:00:17.640
When benzene and bromine are mixed

00:00:17.640 --> 00:00:19.529
they'll move around in solution by

00:00:19.529 --> 00:00:21.750
diffusion, sometimes bumping into each

00:00:21.750 --> 00:00:24.480
other.  But these molecular collisions are

00:00:24.480 --> 00:00:26.970
not going to be productive; they won't

00:00:26.970 --> 00:00:30.000
cause a reaction. Bromine is simply not

00:00:30.000 --> 00:00:32.940
powerful enough—not positive enough—to

00:00:32.940 --> 00:00:36.420
make benzene react with it. However; if we

00:00:36.420 --> 00:00:38.790
add in an iron catalyst this can

00:00:38.790 --> 00:00:41.579
associate with the bromine, building up

00:00:41.579 --> 00:00:45.420
positive charge on it. Now, when benzene

00:00:45.420 --> 00:00:47.640
bumps into this activated bromine

00:00:47.640 --> 00:00:51.449
species it can react. One of the double

00:00:51.449 --> 00:00:53.699
bonds become shared in a new bond with

00:00:53.699 --> 00:00:56.129
bromine and forms a carbocation on

00:00:56.129 --> 00:00:59.550
benzene. Now, one of the bromine atoms can

00:00:59.550 --> 00:01:02.730
dissociate from the iron complex leaving

00:01:02.730 --> 00:01:05.400
as Br minus. This deprotonates the

00:01:05.400 --> 00:01:07.950
molecule, neutralizes the carbocation,

00:01:07.950 --> 00:01:11.430
and forms bromobenzene. Now let's show

00:01:11.430 --> 00:01:13.170
this mechanism with conventional arrow

00:01:13.170 --> 00:01:16.770
pushing. Benzene uses its double bond to

00:01:16.770 --> 00:01:19.710
attack activated bromine and the Br

00:01:19.710 --> 00:01:22.200
minus that's displaced forms a new bond

00:01:22.200 --> 00:01:25.170
with iron. Now the drive for this

00:01:25.170 --> 00:01:27.630
compound to become aromatic again is so

00:01:27.630 --> 00:01:30.329
great that we can show a spontaneous

00:01:30.329 --> 00:01:33.240
deprotonation and just write minus H+

00:01:33.240 --> 00:01:36.329
under the arrow. This reaction is

00:01:36.329 --> 00:01:39.240
general, so anytime benzene encounters an

00:01:39.240 --> 00:01:40.979
electrophile that is powerful enough

00:01:40.979 --> 00:01:43.890
it'll open up its double bond attacking

00:01:43.890 --> 00:01:46.290
it. We can then show the electrons

00:01:46.290 --> 00:01:48.450
pushing back into the ring and if we'd

00:01:48.450 --> 00:01:50.340
like and have an appropriate conjugate

00:01:50.340 --> 00:01:52.920
base in solution we can use that to do a

00:01:52.920 --> 00:01:55.500
deprotonation. This gives us our

00:01:55.500 --> 00:02:00.270
substituted aromatic ring. Now that we've

00:02:00.270 --> 00:02:01.710
looked at the general electrophilic

00:02:01.710 --> 00:02:04.380
aromatic substitution mechanism let's

00:02:04.380 --> 00:02:06.540
look at the possible electrophiles that

00:02:06.540 --> 00:02:10.319
we can add to an aromatic ring. We

00:02:10.319 --> 00:02:12.270
already saw the addition of bromine and

00:02:12.270 --> 00:02:14.040
equivalents of Cl+

00:02:14.040 --> 00:02:17.370
can also be used to react benzene. This

00:02:17.370 --> 00:02:21.329
reaction is halogenation. The nitronium

00:02:21.329 --> 00:02:23.159
ion can react with benzene in the

00:02:23.159 --> 00:02:26.069
nitration reaction. This gives a nitro

00:02:26.069 --> 00:02:29.819
derivative. Sulfonation of benzene can

00:02:29.819 --> 00:02:32.700
be accomplished using sulfur trioxide in

00:02:32.700 --> 00:02:36.060
a solution of sulfuric acid. The

00:02:36.060 --> 00:02:38.519
acylium ion, shown here, can also be

00:02:38.519 --> 00:02:41.280
attacked by benzene. This ion has

00:02:41.280 --> 00:02:43.650
resonance with a carbocation that makes

00:02:43.650 --> 00:02:46.040
it more obvious how benzene will attack.

00:02:46.040 --> 00:02:48.629
The reaction results in the addition of

00:02:48.629 --> 00:02:50.700
an acyl group to benzene and it's called

00:02:50.700 --> 00:02:54.480
Friedel-Crafts alkylation. Finally some

00:02:54.480 --> 00:02:58.760
benzene derivatives can react with carbocations .

00:02:58.760 --> 00:03:01.220
This reaction gives alkylated

00:03:01.220 --> 00:03:02.820
derivatives and is called Friedel-Crafts

00:03:03.000 --> 00:03:04.460
alkylation.

00:03:05.160 --> 00:03:06.880
Benzene is a relatively stable molecule

00:03:06.880 --> 00:03:09.260
However, when it encounters a powerful enough

00:03:09.340 --> 00:03:11.480
electrophile

00:03:11.480 --> 00:03:13.480
It will undergo

00:03:13.480 --> 00:03:15.480
electrophilic aromatic substitution.

00:03:15.480 --> 00:03:17.480
 

00:03:17.480 --> 00:03:19.480
We've also seen the five major benzene

00:03:19.480 --> 00:03:21.480
derivatives that we can form by reactions with

00:03:21.480 --> 00:03:23.480
different electrophiles.

00:03:23.480 --> 00:03:25.480
But we haven't yet looked at how those

00:03:25.480 --> 00:03:27.480
electrophiles are made,

00:03:27.480 --> 00:03:29.480
and the conditions under which these reactions

00:03:29.480 --> 00:03:31.480
occur. We'll do that in a specific

00:03:31.480 --> 00:03:33.480
lesson for each reaction.

00:03:33.480 --> 00:03:35.480
Thanks for watching!

